1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,880
The scent of a rose.

2
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:02,960
Yellow turmeric.

3
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:04,003
A rubber band.

4
00:00:04,403 --> 00:00:08,773
Aarav's mother added a spoon of turmeric
to the dal, and it turned bright

5
00:00:08,773 --> 00:00:09,673
yellow.

6
00:00:09,401 --> 00:00:14,075
On his desk lay a rubber band, made
from the milky sap of a rubber tree.

7
00:00:14,525 --> 00:00:16,172
Aarav wants to be a doctor.

8
00:00:16,172 --> 00:00:19,013
He asked: plants make
colours, scents and rubber.

9
00:00:19,013 --> 00:00:20,920
Do they need them to stay alive?

10
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:26,119
Scientists sort the chemicals of life into
two groups, called primary metabolites

11
00:00:26,119 --> 00:00:27,823
and secondary metabolites.

12
00:00:28,323 --> 00:00:32,822
By the end, you will tell the two apart,
and know eight groups of secondary

13
00:00:32,822 --> 00:00:34,695
metabolites from your textbook.

14
00:00:35,345 --> 00:00:36,678
First, a new word.

15
00:00:37,028 --> 00:00:40,855
Chemists have pulled out thousands
of compounds from living things.

16
00:00:40,855 --> 00:00:43,615
Some are small, like
sugars and amino acids.

17
00:00:43,615 --> 00:00:44,599
Some are big.

18
00:00:45,049 --> 00:00:47,363
These compounds are called metabolites.

19
00:00:47,813 --> 00:00:51,963
Chemists isolate each one, work out
its structure, and, if possible, make

20
00:00:51,963 --> 00:00:52,988
it in the lab.

21
00:00:53,638 --> 00:00:55,997
Let's zoom in, one level at a time.

22
00:00:56,347 --> 00:00:59,851
Start with an animal tissue,
like a piece of liver.

23
00:01:00,351 --> 00:01:01,723
Zoom into its cells.

24
00:01:01,723 --> 00:01:05,259
We find sugars, amino acids,
fatty acids and nucleotides.

25
00:01:05,259 --> 00:01:07,626
These are found in every living thing.

26
00:01:07,626 --> 00:01:09,790
They are called primary metabolites.

27
00:01:10,290 --> 00:01:11,711
Now a plant leaf.

28
00:01:11,711 --> 00:01:13,290
Zoom into its cells.

29
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:16,174
We find all the same primary metabolites.

30
00:01:16,674 --> 00:01:21,440
But we also find thousands of extra
compounds, like pigments, scents and

31
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:22,340
alkaloids.

32
00:01:22,285 --> 00:01:25,873
Plant, fungal and microbial
cells are full of them.

33
00:01:25,873 --> 00:01:28,481
These are called secondary metabolites.

34
00:01:29,131 --> 00:01:34,003
Now watch how a secondary metabolite
reaches us, one step at a time.

35
00:01:34,353 --> 00:01:35,253
Step one.

36
00:01:35,142 --> 00:01:39,192
Take the turmeric plant, and dig
up its yellow underground stem.

37
00:01:39,942 --> 00:01:40,842
Step two.

38
00:01:40,661 --> 00:01:42,020
Dry it and grind it.

39
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:44,396
Thousands of compounds are mixed together.

40
00:01:45,146 --> 00:01:46,046
Step three.

41
00:01:46,004 --> 00:01:48,728
Isolate one yellow
compound from the mixture.

42
00:01:48,728 --> 00:01:50,211
It is called curcumin.

43
00:01:50,961 --> 00:01:51,861
Step four.

44
00:01:51,731 --> 00:01:55,871
Work out its structure, how its atoms
are joined, and if possible, make

45
00:01:55,871 --> 00:01:56,924
it in the lab.

46
00:01:57,674 --> 00:01:58,574
Step five.

47
00:01:58,570 --> 00:01:59,794
Put it to use.

48
00:01:59,794 --> 00:02:02,491
Your textbook lists curcumin as a drug.

49
00:02:02,491 --> 00:02:05,651
Many secondary metabolites
are useful to people.

50
00:02:06,401 --> 00:02:08,900
Secondary metabolites come in many groups.

51
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:11,169
Here are eight, from your textbook.

52
00:02:11,519 --> 00:02:14,089
Pigments, the colours, like
carotenoids and anthocyanins.

53
00:02:14,089 --> 00:02:15,817
Alkaloids, like morphine and codeine.

54
00:02:16,317 --> 00:02:18,231
Terpenoids, like
monoterpenes and diterpenes.

55
00:02:18,231 --> 00:02:19,921
Essential oils, like lemon grass oil.

56
00:02:20,421 --> 00:02:22,105
Toxins, which are poisons,
like abrin and ricin.

57
00:02:22,105 --> 00:02:23,137
Lectins, like concanavalin A.

58
00:02:23,637 --> 00:02:25,849
Drugs, like vinblastin and curcumin.

59
00:02:25,849 --> 00:02:29,768
And polymeric substances, long
chain-like molecules, like rubber, gums

60
00:02:29,768 --> 00:02:30,734
and cellulose.

61
00:02:31,384 --> 00:02:32,538
Side by side.

62
00:02:32,538 --> 00:02:33,438
Where.

63
00:02:33,190 --> 00:02:38,119
Primary metabolites are found in all
living things, like animal tissues.

64
00:02:38,119 --> 00:02:43,261
Secondary metabolites are found mainly
in plant, fungal and microbial cells.

65
00:02:44,011 --> 00:02:44,911
The role.

66
00:02:44,759 --> 00:02:48,309
Primary metabolites have known
roles in normal body processes.

67
00:02:48,309 --> 00:02:52,297
For many secondary metabolites, we
do not yet understand their role in

68
00:02:52,297 --> 00:02:54,123
the organism that makes them.

69
00:02:54,873 --> 00:02:55,773
Examples.

70
00:02:55,517 --> 00:02:57,518
Amino acids and sugars are primary.

71
00:02:57,518 --> 00:02:59,943
Alkaloids, rubber and
scents are secondary.

72
00:03:00,693 --> 00:03:01,593
Your turn.

73
00:03:01,502 --> 00:03:03,271
Concanavalin A is a lectin.

74
00:03:03,271 --> 00:03:06,286
Is it a primary metabolite,
or a secondary one?

75
00:03:06,286 --> 00:03:07,477
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:10,677 --> 00:03:11,577
Secondary!

77
00:03:11,329 --> 00:03:14,174
Lectins are one of the groups
of secondary metabolites.

78
00:03:14,824 --> 00:03:15,779
Remember this.

79
00:03:15,779 --> 00:03:17,421
Primary keeps life running.

80
00:03:17,421 --> 00:03:19,661
Secondary, we are still finding out.

81
00:03:20,261 --> 00:03:21,989
Now, a worked example.

82
00:03:21,989 --> 00:03:23,083
Six compounds.

83
00:03:23,083 --> 00:03:24,596
Primary or secondary?

84
00:03:24,946 --> 00:03:26,175
Glucose, a sugar.

85
00:03:26,175 --> 00:03:27,075
Primary.

86
00:03:27,257 --> 00:03:28,769
Morphine, an alkaloid.

87
00:03:28,769 --> 00:03:29,669
Secondary.

88
00:03:29,971 --> 00:03:32,008
Alanine, an amino acid.

89
00:03:32,008 --> 00:03:32,908
Primary.

90
00:03:33,289 --> 00:03:35,574
Rubber, a polymeric substance.

91
00:03:35,574 --> 00:03:36,474
Secondary.

92
00:03:36,910 --> 00:03:38,784
Palmitic acid, a fatty acid.

93
00:03:38,784 --> 00:03:39,684
Primary.

94
00:03:39,883 --> 00:03:41,972
And anthocyanin, a pigment.

95
00:03:41,972 --> 00:03:42,872
Secondary.

96
00:03:43,494 --> 00:03:44,956
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:03:45,306 --> 00:03:49,208
Many students think secondary
means useless, or not important.

98
00:03:49,608 --> 00:03:54,097
But many secondary metabolites are
useful to people, like rubber, drugs,

99
00:03:54,097 --> 00:03:56,034
spices, scents and pigments.

100
00:03:56,034 --> 00:03:58,780
Some are important for
the environment too.

101
00:03:59,430 --> 00:04:00,800
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:05,533
In board papers, as examples of each
kind, and in NEET, as match the following

103
00:04:05,533 --> 00:04:07,932
with the table of secondary metabolites.

104
00:04:08,532 --> 00:04:09,730
Exam level now.

105
00:04:09,730 --> 00:04:12,076
Match each group with its example.

106
00:04:12,076 --> 00:04:13,325
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:18,725 --> 00:04:19,990
The answer is A.

108
00:04:20,490 --> 00:04:22,414
Codeine is an alkaloid.

109
00:04:22,414 --> 00:04:23,987
Ricin is a toxin.

110
00:04:24,487 --> 00:04:27,417
Concanavalin A is a lectin,
and vinblastin is a drug.

111
00:04:27,417 --> 00:04:28,597
So the match is A.

112
00:04:29,197 --> 00:04:30,737
Here is your recap.

113
00:04:30,737 --> 00:04:32,320
Screenshot this card.

114
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:37,706
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:04:38,106 --> 00:04:39,006
One.

116
00:04:38,573 --> 00:04:41,006
Give two examples of primary metabolites.

117
00:04:41,906 --> 00:04:42,806
Two.

118
00:04:42,390 --> 00:04:44,392
Name one toxin and one lectin.

119
00:04:45,292 --> 00:04:46,192
Three.

120
00:04:45,852 --> 00:04:49,045
Why are rubber and spices
called secondary metabolites?

121
00:04:50,145 --> 00:04:53,341
Next time: why do some molecules
never pass through the filter cloth?

122
00:04:53,341 --> 00:04:55,442
We find out in
biomacromolecules and proteins.
